WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.020
Film Courage: Let’s talk about inconsistency
for creatives, versus the people that are

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:11.589
so on the nose and so concise, but maybe they
have lost some of the creative flair? I happen

00:00:11.589 --> 00:00:15.640
to see a lot of people who are inconsistent
and are incredibly creative and actually have

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:20.141
brilliant ideas, they just don’t follow-up
on them. And on the flipside, the ones who

00:00:20.141 --> 00:00:26.171
actually do follow-up…sometimes the creativity
or flair still needs to be fleshed out. How

00:00:26.171 --> 00:00:27.529
do you nurture both types?

00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:35.239
Lee Jessup: Well you really have to look at
writing as a job rather than a muse, right?

00:00:35.239 --> 00:00:41.000
Larry Karaszewski and Scott Alexander talk
about how they wrote The People and O. J.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:48.030
And Larry F. and a whole bunch of biopics
and bio dramas. And they talk about this is

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:52.640
a job and we go into work even when we don’t
feel it. And some days the writing is great

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.170
and some days it is horrible but it’s the
work.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:58.490
Sometimes you have to write through a lot
of horrible pages to get to some good pages.

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:04.930
I’m a big believer in that. Sometimes you
have to try something different. So it’s

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:12.140
staying agile and open to the different ways
to access creativity. Sometimes it’s rejecting

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:17.140
creativity and just going for inspiration
and through inspiration getting back to creativity.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:21.420
But you have to accept that what works on
one script will likely not work on the next.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:26.400
And so you always, always, always have new
tricks to try in your writing, new ways to

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.171
access the work, new methodology to follow,
new things that will inspire you.

00:01:31.171 --> 00:01:39.320
You know, that if nothing else, you go and
do something for fun because fun begets motivation.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:46.341
So whether your fun is dancing or hiking or
jogging on the beach or whatever it is, you

00:01:46.341 --> 00:01:49.520
usually come back pumped up and excited.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:56.510
That said, creativity is tough to always channel
so a lot of my writers rely on meditation,

00:01:56.510 --> 00:02:02.880
on regular workouts, on tracking systems to
set their expectations for what they are going

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.100
to create and when.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:11.569
A lot of self-journals, a lot of goals and
targeting so that at some point your system

00:02:11.569 --> 00:02:15.410
begins to understand that you have to show
up whether you feel like it or not.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:18.230
Film Courage: What are those tracking systems?
That’s really interesting.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:23.080
Lee Jessup: There is something called SELF
Journal that a lot of my client’s use that

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:29.290
is a planner, a task driven planner with short-term
goals and long-term goals. A lot of writers

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:36.161
rely on systems like Pomodoro that requires
25-minute sessions. So it really varies and

00:02:36.161 --> 00:02:41.030
depends on the writer. But every writer finds
their path. So some writers write for page

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:46.440
count. Some writers write for hour count.
You have to find what is your comfortable

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:51.230
space. To write three pages at a time or six
pages at a time or three hours at a time.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:54.750
What can you meet and what can you meet consistently?
Because it’s kind of like going to the gym.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:58.360
The first few days are going to be really,
really hard. Especially when you haven’t

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:02.530
been in a little while. On day 13, 14, 15
you are going to hit your stride and if you

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:06.170
keep going then you are going to be fine.
And it’s the same with writing. You just

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:11.220
have to keep doing it in order to get comfortable
with it. The beginning is always going to

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:15.219
feel rusty and clunky. By the end, you are
going to be hitting your stride.

